```java
package com.yourgroup.javahub.basics;

/*
 * =========================================================
 * Exercise: Count Vowels
 * =========================================================
 *
 * Objective:
 * Create a method that counts how many vowels are present
 * in a given string.
 *
 * What this exercise practices:
 * - for loops
 * - String traversal
 * - charAt()
 * - Conditional statements
 * - Working with characters
 * - Incrementing counters
 *
 * Your task:
 * Complete the countVowels() method so that it returns
 * the total number of vowels in the input string.
 *
 * Vowels:
 * a, e, i, o, u
 *
 * Examples:
 * Input:  "education"
 * Output: 5
 *
 * Input:  "hello"
 * Output: 2
 *
 * Input:  "rhythm"
 * Output: 0
 *
 * Hints:
 * - Loop through the string one character at a time
 * - Check whether each character is a vowel
 * - Increase the counter when a vowel is found
 *
 * End Goal:
 * Learn how to inspect characters inside a string and
 * keep track of values using counters.
 *
 * =========================================================
 */

public class CountVowels {

    public static int countVowels(String input) {
        return 0;
    }
}
```

Test class:

```java
package com.yourgroup.javahub.basics;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

class CountVowelsTest {

    @Test
    void shouldCountAllVowels() {
        assertEquals(5, CountVowels.countVowels("education"));
    }

    @Test
    void shouldCountSomeVowels() {
        assertEquals(2, CountVowels.countVowels("hello"));
    }

    @Test
    void shouldReturnZeroWhenNoVowelsExist() {
        assertEquals(0, CountVowels.countVowels("rhythm"));
    }

    @Test
    void shouldWorkWithSingleLetter() {
        assertEquals(1, CountVowels.countVowels("a"));
    }ffff
}
```

This one is REALLY good because it introduces a super important beginner pattern:

```text
loop → inspect → condition → increment counter
```

which appears EVERYWHERE in programming.
